    <header class="hero">
      <span class="eyebrow">Investment Basics Explained Simply</span>
      <h1>How a Corporate Action is Processed</h1>
      <p class="lead">We've explained *what* corporate actions are. Now, let's look at *how* they actually happen. Think of this like a factory assembly line. From a CEO announcing a dividend on TV, to the exact moment the $50 lands in your account, this is the 7-step journey every event takes to ensure no one loses their money.</p>
      <div class="hero-tags">
        <span>7 clear steps</span>
        <span>Everyday analogies</span>
        <span>How mistakes are caught</span>
        <span>Real-world examples</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ============ INTRO ============ -->
    <section id="intro">
      <h2 class="section-title">The Big Picture: The Telephone Game</h2>
      <p>Imagine the CEO of Apple announces they are paying a $1 dividend. They don't just open a banking app and hit "Send" to millions of users.</p>
      <p>Instead, that information has to travel down a long chain. Apple tells a giant central bank, who tells a global bank, who tells your broker (like Robinhood or Fidelity), who finally puts the money in your account. <strong>It's like playing a giant game of Telephone with billions of dollars.</strong></p>
      <p>The biggest problem the financial industry faces isn't doing the math—it's making sure the message doesn't get messed up as it gets passed down the chain. That is why this 7-step "Assembly Line" exists.</p>
      
      <div class="stat-grid">
        <div class="stat-card"><span class="stat-num">100+</span><span class="stat-label">The number of different places a bank might receive news about a *single* event, which they have to cross-check.</span></div>
        <div class="stat-card"><span class="stat-num">$400M</span><span class="stat-label">The estimated amount of money Wall Street wastes every year just fixing mistakes caused by bad communication during these events.</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 7 STAGES ============ -->
    <div class="part-heading"><h2>The 7 Stages of the Assembly Line</h2><span class="part-count">From Announcement to Payout</span></div>
    <section id="stages">

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-1">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">01</span><div><h3>The Announcement</h3><p class="stage-tag">Where it starts (Outside the bank)</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">The company's Board of Directors makes a decision and announces it to the world. "We are going to pay a $1 dividend next Friday!"</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>The company puts out a press release, tells the stock exchange, or files legal paperwork. The news hits financial websites like Bloomberg or Yahoo Finance.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Why it's tricky</h4><p>Banks and brokers are suddenly flooded with news alerts. Some alerts say the dividend is $1.00, some say it's $1.005. Everyone is scrambling to figure out the exact truth.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="breaks-flag"><b>What can go wrong:</b> Because the information is flying everywhere, you can't trust the very first news alert you read. You have to wait for the official paperwork.</div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-2">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">02</span><div><h3>Fact Checking (The Golden Record)</h3><p class="stage-tag">Finding the one true version of the facts</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">Before your broker tells you about the event, their back-office team (the Operations Team) aggressively fact-checks the news.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>They compare the news alert against the official stock exchange documents. Once they are 100% sure the dividend is exactly $1.00 per share, and the deadline is exactly Friday at 5 PM, they lock it in. This verified information is called the <span class="highlight-term">Golden Record</span>.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="breaks-flag"><b>What can go wrong:</b> If the team gets the math wrong here (e.g., they mistakenly record the dividend as $10 instead of $1), it will cause massive problems later because every calculation will be based on that typo.</div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-3">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">03</span><div><h3>Sending the Invites</h3><p class="stage-tag">Notifying the investors</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">Now that the broker knows the exact facts (the Golden Record), they send an alert or an email to every single customer who owns the stock.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>You get an email saying: "Apple has announced a dividend. Here is what you need to know." If it's an event where you have to make a choice (like a vote or a buyback), they give you a deadline.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="breaks-flag"><b>What can go wrong:</b> Sometimes, emails get delayed or lost in spam. If you don't read the email, you might miss your chance to make a choice.</div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-4">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">04</span><div><h3>Doing the Math (Entitlement)</h3><p class="stage-tag">Figuring out who gets what</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">The broker looks at their system to see exactly how many shares you own on the "Record Date" (The VIP Guest List date). They use a simple formula to figure out what they owe you.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>If you own 100 shares, and the Golden Record says the dividend is $1.00, they calculate: <strong>100 x $1.00 = $100.</strong> They write this down on a master list so they know exactly what to pay you on Friday.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="breaks-flag"><b>What can go wrong:</b> If you bought your shares at the very last second, the system might accidentally leave you off the VIP list. (This causes a "Market Claim" to fix it).</div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card v-voluntary" id="stage-5">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">05</span><div><h3>Getting the RSVPs</h3><p class="stage-tag">Only happens if you have to make a choice</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">If the event requires you to vote or choose how you want to be paid, the broker waits for your response.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>You click a button in your app to make your choice. The broker collects the choices of millions of users, bundles them up, and sends one giant "Master RSVP" to the company.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="breaks-flag"><b>What can go wrong:</b> <strong>Missing the deadline.</strong> If you forget to vote, the company will just apply their "default" choice to your account, which might not be what you wanted. Deadlines are strict; a minute late is too late.</div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-6">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">06</span><div><h3>Paying Out (Execution)</h3><p class="stage-tag">The money actually moves</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">Payday has arrived. The company sends millions of dollars to the broker, and the broker distributes it into all the individual user accounts.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>Your cash balance goes up by $100, or you see new shares appear in your portfolio. The event has officially happened.</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="stage-card" id="stage-7">
        <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">07</span><div><h3>Checking the Receipts (Reconciliation)</h3><p class="stage-tag">Making sure nobody was shortchanged</p></div></div>
        <p class="stage-def">The final, most important step. The broker double-checks their math to make sure the amount of money they received from the company perfectly matches what they were expecting.</p>
        <div class="ca-grid">
          <div><h4>What happens</h4><p>They look at the math they did in Stage 4 (We expected $100). They look at the receipt from Stage 6 (We received $100). If the numbers match perfectly, the event is closed. If the numbers don't match (a "Break"), an alarm goes off and a human has to investigate where the missing money went.</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </section>

    <!-- ============ KEY CONCEPTS ============ -->
    <div class="part-heading"><h2>Important Concepts (How to avoid traps)</h2><span class="part-count">Behind the scenes logic</span></div>
    
    <section id="three-way">
      <h2 class="section-title">The 3-Way Check (Reconciliation)</h2>
      <p>In Stage 7, banks don't just check two numbers; they check <strong>three</strong>. Think of it like making sure your restaurant bill is correct.</p>
      
      <ol class="lifecycle">
        <li><h4>1. What you expected to pay</h4><p>You ordered a $10 burger. You expect the bill to be $10. (This is the math the broker did in Stage 4).</p></li>
        <li><h4>2. What the waiter says you owe</h4><p>The waiter brings the bill, and it says $10. (This is the notification from the mega-bank).</p></li>
        <li><h4>3. What actually left your wallet</h4><p>You check your credit card app, and it shows a charge of $10. (This is the final receipt from Stage 6).</p></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout teal">
        <h4>Why do we check 3 things? To find out *who* made the mistake!</h4>
        <p>If you expect a $10 bill, the waiter asks for $10, but your credit card was charged $15, you know the restaurant's credit card machine is broken (A settlement error). <br><br>But if you expect a $10 bill, and the waiter asks for $15, you know the waiter wrote down the wrong order (A data error). Checking three points tells the bank exactly who to call to fix the missing money.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="cutoffs">
      <h2 class="section-title">The Deadline Trap</h2>
      <p>If a company announces that you have until Friday at 5:00 PM to vote on a merger, your broker will likely tell you that <strong>your</strong> deadline is Thursday at 5:00 PM. Why did they steal a day from you?</p>
      <p>Because they have to play the Telephone game backward. Your broker needs a day to gather all 5 million user votes, package them up, and mail them to the mega-bank. The mega-bank needs a day to gather votes from 50 different brokers and mail them to the company. </p>
      <div class="callout red">
        <h4>Warning</h4>
        <p>This is why you can never wait until the absolute last minute. If you miss your broker's internal deadline, it doesn't matter if the company's official deadline is still hours away. Your broker won't be able to process it in time.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    
    <section id="manufactured">
      <h2 class="section-title">What if my shares are out on loan?</h2>
      <p>Many people don't realize this, but brokers often lend out your shares to other traders (short sellers) to make extra money. What happens if a company pays a dividend while a stranger is borrowing your shares?</p>
      <p>The stranger receives the real dividend from the company. However, by law, the stranger has to instantly pull money out of their own pocket and pay you an exact replica of the dividend. This is called a <span class="highlight-term">Manufactured Payment</span>. To you, it looks exactly like a normal dividend, but the tax rules for it can sometimes be slightly different.</p>
    </section>

